From 129b420286a763749fae23ed3f5498f521c03834 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vortiago <atle@havso.net>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 15:12:28 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(ggml-cpu): run tiny serial op runs on thread 0 without
 barriers
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The graph compute loop puts a threadpool barrier after every node. At
decode most nodes are tiny element-wise ops (gates, router softmax and
normalise, norms, bookkeeping), so the barriers cost more than the work.

A node now runs on thread 0 alone, with nth = 1, when its op is on a
whitelist of row-wise ops that never call ggml_barrier or the threadpool
chunk counter, and when its data is at most GGML_CPU_SERIAL_BYTES bytes
(default 32768, 0 disables it). The other threads skip the node. Inside a
run of such nodes there is no barrier. One barrier stays after the last
node of the run. Every thread evaluates the same predicate on the graph,
so all threads agree on where the barriers are.

The whitelisted ops split their work by rows or elements with no
reduction across threads, so the result is bit-exact for any thread
count. Ops with an internal barrier (mul_mat, mul_mat_id, flash_attn_ext,
gated_delta_net, out_prod, acc, set, diag_mask, count_equal, conv and
conv_transpose, add_rel_pos, flash_attn_back, rwkv, gla, cross_entropy)
are not on the whitelist. An RMS_NORM that fuses with the next MUL runs
serially only when the MUL also fits.

Thread 0 checks the abort callback only at the end of a run, before the
kept barrier. The other threads may already have passed any node inside
the run, so an abort index inside the run would make them miss the stop
and wait at a barrier that thread 0 never reaches.

The reference path (use_ref) keeps the old behaviour.

